#1  More Calculus (yay!)
===============================================================================================

  Let's say:  y  =  f (x)

  Then let's say we increase x by an infinitesimal amount dx -- y will receive a corresponding variation dy (increase or decrease,
  depending on f).

  Then:  f (x + dx)  =  y + dy  =  f (x)  +  dy    <=>    dy  =  f (x + dx)  -  f (x)

  Let's call back our snail from Chapter II.
  If x is time and y is distance, then dy is: how much distance the snail has covered during the time dx.
  So dy is a distance. Its value is dependant upon the duration of dx.
  So if we want the rate of change of y (the speed of the snail), we need to normalise dy relatively to dx.
  That is: we don't want dy, we want dy per dx -- whose shortened notation is:  dy / dx.
  The differential operator "d" is both in the numerator and the denominator, so it cancels out. Which is why we're allowed to define
  speed as "distance over time" without mention of infinitesimal increase (don't quote this one).

                                                  dy       f (x + dx)  -  f (x)
  If:    dy  =  f (x + dx)  -  f (x)    then:    ----  =  ----------------------
                                                  dx                dx

  Now let's define g (x) so that  f (x)  =  α g (x)  --  α being a constant.
  Let's also define  df = dy so that we can distinguish it from dg (the variation of g (x) corresponding to an increase of x by dx).

  Then:  dy  =  df  =  α g (x + dx)  -  α g (x)  =  α ( g (x + dx)  -  g (x) )  =  α dg

  In what precedes, I've been taking for granted that f (x) and g (x) have no discontinuity and share the same range of definition
  (domain). It's actually not necessarily granted. If it's not true, this will break our equations. Which is another way of saying
  that continuity and equal domain of definition are the conditions for our equations to be true. These equations prove the constant
  factor rule. This paragraph proves the conditions under which said rule is valid.


  Ok, let's start over and have:  h (x)  =  f ( g (x) )

  Then:  dh  =  h (x + dx)  -  h (x)
             =  f ( g (x)  +  dg )  -  f ( g (x) )
             =  f ( g (x)  +  g (x + dx)  -  g (x) )  -  f ( g (x) )
             =  f ( g (x + dx) )  -  f ( g (x) )

  Yeah... not helping. Dimensional analysis?
  Maybe stick to the speed example. Let's name stuff: t is time, D is distance and v is speed (dD / dt).
  Or: t is the input variable, D is the output and v is the rate of change.
  Then:

    v  =  D / t    <=>    D  =  v * t    <=>    t  =  D / v

  Rate of change is output over input (1). Output is rate of change times input (2). Input is output over rate of change (3).
